LEAD = {
    "headline": "INSTAGRAM RAISED ITS VIDEO CEILING FROM 20 MINUTES TO 90, PUT IT ON APPLE TV, AND SAID IT IS TOO EARLY TO TALK ABOUT PAYING FOR IT",
    "deck": "Instagram briefed press yesterday on Features, horizontal video up to 90 minutes, with up to eight Reels that link back to the full thing.  Adam Mosseri has spent years saying he did not want long-form on Instagram.  The part nobody is leading with is the money, because there is not any — Meta says it is too early to talk about how creators get paid for it.  Which tells you exactly who is expected to fund long-form video on Instagram.",
    "stamps": [
        ("TECHCRUNCH · 26 OCT", "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/26/watch-out-youtube-instagram-goes-from-short-form-to-90-minute-videos/"),
        ("FAST COMPANY · 26 OCT", "https://www.fastcompany.com/91630089/why-instagram-is-about-to-look-a-lot-more-like-youtube"),
        ("THE NATIONAL · 26 OCT", "https://www.thenationalnews.com/future/technology/2026/10/26/instagram-video-length-adam-mosseri/"),
        ("TUBEFILTER · IGTV, 2021", "https://www.tubefilter.com/2021/10/06/instagram-is-sunsetting-its-igtv-video-format/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "The product first, because the shape of it matters more than the headline number.  Features is <mark>horizontal video up to 90 minutes</mark>, against a previous ceiling of <mark>20 minutes</mark> on Reels.  Alongside it, a creator can post <mark>up to eight Reels that link directly back to the source video</mark>.  Features also becomes a core part of Instagram for TV, which The National reports is <mark>now on Apple TV in the United States</mark>.  Testing started with long-form creators in <mark>Brazil</mark>, moved to select United States creators, and TechCrunch reports it reaching <mark>tens of thousands of creators in the United States over the coming months</mark>.",
        "Mosseri, head of Instagram, on his own record here: <mark>for a long time, I have been against doing long-form video on Instagram</mark>.  He says he is trying to make it so that the short-form and the long-form videos can be symbiotic.  Tessa Lyons, vice president of product, on why the TV app forced it: we know that people like to get together and actually watch Reels and video together, and we see this happening on TV, so we've been working on a TV version of Reels and we know that to do that well, <mark>we need more than just short-form video</mark>.",
        "Now the money, which is the story.  Asked how creators get paid for a 90-minute video, a Meta spokesperson told TechCrunch: <mark>it's too early to share a long-term monetization model</mark>.  No per-view payout.  No revenue share announced.  Mosseri's framing of creator income is three buckets — you can get paid by the platform directly, you can get paid by a fan, and you can get paid by a brand — and on the first of those he says of Instagram's existing performance-based programme that <mark>we spend more money than we make back by a long shot</mark>.",
        "Hold that against what the incumbent pays.  YouTube gives Partner Programme creators <mark>55 percent of the advertising revenue</mark> on their videos, and says it has paid out <mark>more than 100 billion dollars</mark> to creators, artists and media companies over the previous four years.  YouTube advertising alone brought in <mark>more than 11 billion dollars between April and June this year, up 13 percent</mark>.  So Instagram is opening a 90-minute slot against a competitor that pays a fixed share of a very large number, and it is opening it with no share at all.",
        "Which leaves one source of money for long-form Instagram video, and it is the people reading this.  If the platform does not pay for the format, every minute of it that gets made gets made because a brand paid for it, or because a creator is using it as a shop window for something else.  That is not a criticism of the product.  It is the commercial reality of the product, and it is unusually clear this early.",
        "The reason to not treat this as a done deal — Instagram has run this exact play before.  <mark>IGTV launched in 2018</mark> with long-form uploads and its own app, and Tubefilter reported Instagram <mark>sunsetting the IGTV format in October 2021</mark>.  The difference this time is the TV app and the eight linking Reels, which is a genuine distribution mechanism rather than a separate destination nobody visited.  But the thing that killed IGTV was that creators had no reason to make for it, and the payout question is still open.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("90 min", "new horizontal video ceiling on Instagram Features, up from 20 minutes on Reels"),
        ("8", "Reels a creator can post that link directly back to one long-form video"),
        ("55 pct", "share of advertising revenue YouTube gives Partner Programme creators, against no announced share on Instagram Features"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "There is no Instagram blog post or Mosseri post behind this — it came from a press briefing with Mosseri and Tessa Lyons, so every detail is second-hand through the outlets that attended, and they do not fully agree.  Fast Company says a limited rollout starts this month; The National says the feature arrives in the weeks ahead.  Fast Company gives the eight-linking-Reels figure and the former 20-minute ceiling; The National gives neither, and only The National reports the Apple TV availability.  The Brazil test and the United States creator test are attributed to The Hollywood Reporter, which this brief has not read directly.  No creator count, no United States date and no Features watch-time or engagement figure exists anywhere.  The 55 percent share and the 100 billion dollar payout figure are YouTube's own published numbers, cited by TechCrunch for contrast rather than measured against anything on Instagram.  Treat the IGTV comparison as this brief's reading of a pattern, not as a prediction either outlet made.",
    "so_what": "A platform just created a 90-minute brand-video slot and told you in the same breath that it is not funding the content that goes in it.  That makes the economics of long-form Instagram video completely different from long-form YouTube, where a creator has a baseline income from the platform and your money is a top-up.  On Instagram there is no baseline, so a creator pitching you a long-form series is pricing it as the entire revenue for that work.  Expect quotes that look high against a YouTube integration and are actually rational.",
    "do_this": "Ask the two creators you work with most whether they are in the Features test, and if either is, buy one long-form episode now while there is no benchmark price and no competition for the slot.  Price it as production rather than as media, because that is what it is.  And whatever you make, treat the eight linking Reels as the deliverable that carries the work — write them into the contract as named, separately delivered assets rather than as cutdowns, because on this platform the clip is the distribution and the long video is the destination.",
}

